          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "cdc17dba-734f-393e-9fe0-60468f1948c9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker to predict future random numbers generated by the program. This can lead to a variety of security issues, such as predictable session identifiers, passwords, or cryptographic keys.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is the use of the `shuffle` function with a non-cryptographic random number generator (`m_state.mt[i568]`).\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `<random>` library, which provides a variety of random number generators that are suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::shuffle(m_state.mt.begin(), m_state.mt.end(), gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to seed the Mersenne Twister engine (`std::mt19937`), which is a cryptographically secure random number generator. Then, `std::shuffle` is used with this generator to shuffle the `m_state.mt` array.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c4a255bd-1b18-33b3-a289-7dfa927552b8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable patterns in the generated numbers, which can be exploited by attackers to predict future numbers or even the entire state of the generator. This can lead to serious security issues, especially in cryptographic contexts where the unpredictability of random numbers is crucial.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::random_device` class from the `<random>` library, which is designed to produce non-deterministic random numbers. If a deterministic sequence is required, `std::mt19937` (Mersenne Twister) can be used, but it should be properly seeded with `std::random_device`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the weak random number generator with a cryptographically secure one. Here is an example of how to do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\n// Create a random device\nstd::random_device rd;\n\n// Use it to seed a Mersenne Twister generator\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\n\n// Generate a random number\nint random_number = gen();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f4282d0f-5491-39c8-aa21-b26bdbf401f3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker to predict the outcome of the random number generator, leading to a potential security breach.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `shuffle(m_state.mt[i340_4], 4)` is a weak random number generator as it does not provide cryptographically secure random numbers.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `<random>` library which provides a variety of random number generators that are suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the given code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(1, 6);\n\ni340 = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister pseudo-random generator of 32-bit numbers with a state size of 19937 bits. `std::uniform_int_distribution` produces random integers in a range [a, b].\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
