            {
              "id": "216d4a70-490a-3a01-98d6-537d96d1f99a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a C++ program. Cryptographic hash functions are used to ensure the integrity and security of data. However, if a weak or broken hash function like SHA-1 is used, it can lead to various security issues such as collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to unauthorized access, data corruption, and other security breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger hash function such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and are not known to have any significant vulnerabilities. Additionally, it is important to keep up to date with the latest cryptographic research and standards to ensure that the hash functions used remain secure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of the SHA-1 hash function with a stronger hash function. For example, if you are using the OpenSSL library, you can replace the use of the SHA1 function with the SHA256 function.\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\n// ...\n\nunsigned char poly_sha1[21];\n// becomes\nunsigned char poly_sha256[33];\n\n// ...\n\nSHA1(poly, sizeof(poly), poly_sha1);\n// becomes\nSHA256(poly, sizeof(poly), poly_sha256);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe OpenSSL library is required for the SHA-1 and SHA-256 hash functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "082fda64-9c5e-3e97-a72f-d24fb003ee78",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker to predict the outcome of the random number generator, leading to a breach of security.\n\nNon-cryptographic random number generators are designed for statistical randomness, not unpredictability. They are often initialized with predictable seeds, such as the system time, making it easier for an attacker to guess the seed and predict future random numbers.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically secure random number generator. These generators are designed to be unpredictable and resistant to knowing the next number in the sequence even if complete state of the generator is known.\n\nIn C++, you can use the `<random>` library which provides a variety of random number generators that are cryptographically secure. For example, you can use `std::random_device` to generate a non-deterministic random number, or `std::mt19937` (Mersenne Twister 19937) for deterministic random numbers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `shuffle` function with a cryptographically secure random number generator. Here is an example of how to do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 g(rd());\n\nstd::shuffle(m_state.mt[i283_3].begin(), m_state.mt[i283_3].end(), g);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
